course: dictionary
lesson: 10521
title: A machine for writing
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A typewriter is a machine for writing. You press keys, and small metal letters hit an inked ribbon and print onto paper. The context helps you: «an old typewriter» tells you it is not a modern computer.
explain: Read the words around an unknown word. In «Enigma, a typewriter-like machine whose turning rotors changed every letter», the word «like» is a clue. Something that is typewriter-like is similar to a typewriter, so it has keys and prints letters, but it is not quite one.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. In «The QWERTY order of keys comes from typewriters of the 1870s», put in "a machine for writing" for typewriters. The sentence still makes sense, so your meaning is right.
fact: A typewriter is a machine for writing: you press keys and letters print on paper.
fact: A typewriter ribbon is the inked strip that the letters hit to make a print.
fact: Typewriters were used in offices and homes before computers became common.
fact: The QWERTY order of keys on many keyboards comes from typewriters of the 1870s.
fact: A pangram is a sentence that uses every letter of the alphabet, such as «The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog», and it was used for testing typewriters.
exercise: «The children found an old typewriter in the attic.» What is a typewriter? | *A machine for writing, with keys that print letters on paper | A toy car with wheels | A tin of paint for walls
exercise: Where were typewriters used before computers became common? | *Offices and homes | Under the sea in submarines | Space rockets
exercise: «Enigma, a typewriter-like machine, changed every letter.» What does typewriter-like mean? | Louder than a bell | *Similar to a typewriter | Made of wood and glass for cooking
test: What is the inked strip called that the letters hit to print? | Keyboard | Pencil lead | *Typewriter ribbon | Paper clip
test: Which keyboard layout comes from typewriters? | *QWERTY | Numbers in a row | Alphabetical order
exam: Why might a story use a typewriter? | *To suggest an old-fashioned writer or office with clacking keys | To describe a fish swimming in the sea | To show a rocket landing on the moon
